{"kind": "report", "step": 14, "ts": "2026-11-18T01:39:31.646335+00:00", "data": {"category": "dns", "host": "dns1.lan", "component": "primary-dns rate limiter (ratelimit: 20 q/s per /24)", "cause": "The primary DNS service on dns1.lan enforces an aggressive per-subnet rate limit (20 q/s for 10.20.0.0/24) that intermittently drops legitimate client queries during brief traffic bursts, and because the backup resolver on gw.lan suppresses the private-address answers for .lan names (rebinding protection with private-address: 10.0.0.0/8), clients get no answer at all until the burst ends \u2014 producing transient \"getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND\" alerts for local names while internet names (resolved elsewhere) are unaffected.", "evidence": ["mon.lan journal shows alerts exactly at burst times: 'Mar 03 09:41:00 monitor[812]: [media] WARN getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND media.lan' and 'Mar 03 13:02:00 ... ENOTFOUND nas.lan', each clearing within 1-2 minutes (journalctl -n 40).", "dns1.lan journal for the same process shows matching events: 'ratelimit: 10.20.0.0/24 exceeded 20 q/s, dropping 23/31/19 queries' at 02:14, 09:41, and 13:02 \u2014 timestamps line up one-to-one with the monitor's ENOTFOUND alerts (journalctl -u primary-dns).", "/opt/primary-dns/config.yaml sets ratelimit: 20 with ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4: 24, far below what a whole /24 of clients can generate in a burst.", "dig @10.20.0.3 nas.lan succeeds normally (NOERROR, A 10.20.0.50, TTL 10), proving the zone data itself is fine when not rate-limited.", "dig @10.20.0.1 nas.lan returns NOERROR with ANSWER: 0 \u2014 the backup resolver never yields an address; gw.lan /var/unbound/unbound.conf forwards lan. to 10.20.0.3 yet also applies private-address: 10.0.0.0/8 rebinding protection, so rewritten private answers are suppressed, leaving no usable fallback path (drill nas.lan @127.0.0.1 on gw.lan shows the same empty answer)."], "action": "change_config", "recommendation": "Raise or disable the rate limit on dns1.lan's primary-dns (e.g. increase ratelimit well above normal aggregate LAN traffic or exempt the trusted 10.20.0.0/24 subnet) so legitimate bursts aren't dropped. Secondarily, stop the rebinding protection on gw.lan from hiding the .lan answers (private-address filtering currently suppresses the forwarded 10.20.0.50 replies) so the second DNS server can serve local names as a real fallback. No reboot or hardware change is needed \u2014 these are configuration adjustments."}}
